WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.600
I recognize that some people feel diminished by geologic time—that humans

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.780
are made insignificant by it. And I can understand that. I think it helps to

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:12.840
start developing some depth of field in
thinking about time. I had a math teacher

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:17.130
who was fond of saying, 'There are many sizes and shapes of infinity.' And while

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:22.199
geologic time is not infinite, it is long.
Some past events like the Pleistocene,

00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:26.789
the Ice Age, happened just the other day. Others, say the formation of Pangaea, a

00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:32.940
while ago. And some, the origin of life
for instance, a long time back. It's also

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:38.040
useful to adopt the Greek distinction
between 'chronos'—raw time billions and

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:43.620
billions of years—and 'kairos,' which is
time within a narrative. As one starts

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.879
knowing Earth's story, and those vast
stretches of geologic time are filled

00:00:47.879 --> 00:00:52.050
with narratives, protagonists and plot lines, it's no longer alienating. The

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:56.399
characters become familiar, the themes resonant. Once we open a few volumes in

00:00:56.399 --> 00:01:00.149
the library of deep time, we can begin to recognize ourselves in the tales of

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:04.680
earlier Earthlings. Indeed, I find the
idea of a seven or eight thousand year

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.299
old Earth—with a shallow, short history—
positively terrifying, while the

00:01:09.299 --> 00:01:13.290
enveloping presence of so many ancestors, with such a rich anthology anthology of

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:18.320
life stories to share, is to me deeply
comforting.

